INT. LAKESIDE BOATHOUSE BALLROOM - NIGHT

A long timber hall on stilts above black water. Faded bunting. A chandelier with half its bulbs out. Through tall windows, the lake lies glassy and still under a quarter moon. A gramophone in the corner plays a thin, crackling waltz.

Four people occupy the room, and only one of them is dancing.

ELOWEN TREGARTHEN-ADEYEMI (30s, sea-green dress, bare shoulders) turns slowly in the center of the floor, alone, her eyes half closed. At the bar, ARTEMIS KOWALCZYK-MWANGI (30s, rolled-up shirt sleeves, a bottle in one hand) pours two glasses and pours a third. LEONID BRAUNSTEIN-OKAFOR (50s, dinner jacket, white gloves) stands near the gramophone with a hand on the horn, as if holding the music in place. And on the window seat, curled like a cat, sits a girl of eight, MIRABEL (small, in a nightgown, hair loose), who is not supposed to be awake.

She spins. The hem of her dress sweeps the boards. She stops, breathless, and looks directly at him.

ELOWEN
You never asked me to dance.

He lowers the bottle. He does not answer. She walks toward him, slowly, leaving a track in the dust.

ARTEMIS
I don't dance.

ELOWEN
You did once.

ARTEMIS
That was a different year.

ELOWEN
It was this year.

Leonid clears his throat. Both glance at him.

LEONID
Perhaps I should adjust the record.

ELOWEN
Leave it.

He lifts his hand from the horn, relieved. Artemis puts down the bottle. He steps out from behind the bar. For a moment they stand a yard apart.

ARTEMIS
The lake is still.

ELOWEN
It always is, here.

ARTEMIS
That frightens me.

ELOWEN
Does it?

ARTEMIS
You don't know what's under it.

She smiles at that, the first smile of the night, and takes his hand. He lets her. She pulls him gently onto the floor, one arm round his shoulder, the other in his palm, and they sway to the gramophone's crooked waltz. At the window, Mirabel hugs her knees.

LEONID
(softly)
Lovely.

MIRABEL
(also softly)
He's going to cry.

LEONID
Who?

MIRABEL
Him.

Leonid peers. Artemis's face is turned to the dark window, jaw set, eyes bright. He does not cry. Not yet. He holds her closer.

ARTEMIS
I couldn't stay.

ELOWEN
I know.

ARTEMIS
I couldn't tell you why.

ELOWEN
I know that too.

The record stutters. Leonid lunges for the needle. The room goes still and quiet, and Elowen stops, her hand still in Artemis's, her eyes on his face.

ELOWEN (CONT'D)
Say it, then.

ARTEMIS
Say what?

ELOWEN
Whatever it is you came all this way to say.

He opens his mouth. Closes it. From the window seat comes the dry rustle of Mirabel unfolding, and then her small, bare feet on the boards, crossing the room, stopping beside them.

MIRABEL
He wants you to come back.

Silence. Then the lake, at last, ripples, once, and a single bulb of the chandelier pops.

ELOWEN
Does he?

ARTEMIS
(barely)
Yes.

Elowen releases his hand. Steps back. She looks at the child, at Leonid, at the bottle on the bar, and then at Artemis again, and for a long moment says nothing at all.

Then she slaps him. The crack rings through the hall. The gramophone, which Leonid has been holding down with both hands, begins, absurdly, to play again.

ELOWEN
(trembling)
Eleven years.

ARTEMIS
I know.

ELOWEN
Eleven years, and you send a child.

MIRABEL
I wasn't sent. I came.

Artemis laughs, a wet, helpless sound. Elowen turns away toward the dark windows, her back straight. Leonid, with great delicacy, picks up the third glass and offers it to her. She takes it. She drinks it in one swallow.
